        'modules' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Setting Up a Professional Teletherapy Practice',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A successful teletherapy practice begins well before the first session. Clinicians need a stable, high-bandwidth internet connection, a HIPAA-compliant video platform (such as a dedicated teletherapy platform rather than generic consumer video chat), a quiet and well-lit room, and a backup communication plan (phone number or secondary platform) in case the connection drops. Equipment matters too: an external microphone and a camera positioned at eye level improve the client\'s ability to see articulatory placement and facial expression, both of which are often central to speech therapy.</p><p>Licensure is a frequently overlooked barrier. Speech-language pathologists must hold an active license in the state where the client is physically located at the time of service, not only where the clinician is based. Many states participate in interstate compacts or offer telehealth-specific registrations, but requirements vary and change, so clinicians must verify current rules with their state licensing board before treating a client across state lines.</p><p>Finally, informed consent for teletherapy should explicitly cover the technology being used, privacy limitations of remote service delivery, and a contingency plan for technical failure, documented separately from general treatment consent.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A client\'s family frequently has unstable internet and sits in a noisy, shared living room. What specific accommodations would you propose, and at what point would you recommend discontinuing teletherapy in favor of in-person services?']],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Engagement Strategies for Video-Based Sessions',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Maintaining a young child\'s attention through a screen requires deliberate adaptation, not simply moving in-person activities online. Screen-share-based interactive games, digital manipulatives, and shared-screen drawing tools can replicate the turn-taking and joint attention that physical materials provide. Pacing should be brisk, with frequent novelty and movement breaks, since video sessions are more fatiguing to sustain attention through than face-to-face interaction.</p><p>Caregiver involvement becomes essential rather than optional in pediatric teletherapy. Because the clinician cannot physically prompt or position the child, the caregiver often serves as the clinician\'s hands in the room, implementing cues the therapist models and narrates in real time. This requires the clinician to shift part of their skill set toward coaching adults, giving concise in-the-moment feedback, and building caregiver confidence rather than directly executing every technique themselves.</p><p>For older students and adults, using shared documents, visual supports, and screen annotation tools keeps sessions interactive rather than lecture-style, which is important for sustaining engagement in language therapy, cognitive-communication work, or fluency treatment.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                        ['question' => 'For a 4-year-old receiving teletherapy, who should typically implement hands-on prompts or physical positioning during the session?', 'options' => [['text' => 'The caregiver, coached in real time by the clinician', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'The clinician, through the screen', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'No prompting should occur in teletherapy', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'The child\'s sibling', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Since the clinician cannot physically touch or position the child remotely, the caregiver becomes the implementer of physical prompts, guided live by the clinician\'s instructions.'],
                        ['question' => 'Why are video sessions often more fatiguing for young children than in-person sessions?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Sustained screen attention lacks the physical and sensory cues of in-person interaction', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Video sessions are always longer in duration', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Screens emit more light than room lighting', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Children prefer in-person sessions', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Two-dimensional, screen-mediated interaction removes many nonverbal and physical cues that support attention, making sustained focus more effortful.'],
                        ['question' => 'What is a key adaptation for keeping an adult client engaged in a teletherapy language session?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Using shared documents and screen annotation for interactivity', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Relying solely on verbal instruction without visuals', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Extending session length to compensate for lower engagement', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Avoiding any technology beyond video', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Interactive, visual tools keep the session collaborative rather than passive, which supports engagement in remote language and cognitive-communication therapy.'],
