            [
                'title' => 'Confidentiality and Documentation in Remote Practice',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Confidentiality obligations do not relax for remote service delivery; if anything, they require more active management. Clinicians must verify that the platform used is compliant with applicable privacy regulations (such as HIPAA in the United States), that sessions are conducted from a private space where conversations cannot be overheard, and that screen-sharing never inadvertently exposes another client\'s file or an open email inbox.</p><p>On the client side, clinicians should ask at the start of each session who else is present in the room, since siblings, other family members, or household staff may be within earshot or camera view. This is both a confidentiality and a clinical consideration, since an unexpected audience can affect a client\'s willingness to participate, particularly in sensitive areas like fluency or voice therapy.</p><p>Documentation should note the platform used, confirm informed consent for telehealth was obtained, and record any technical disruptions that affected service delivery, since this protects both client care continuity and the clinician in the event of an audit or dispute.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'assignment', 'content' => ['instructions' => '<p>Draft a telehealth-specific informed consent paragraph (150-200 words) that you would add to a standard treatment consent form, covering platform privacy limitations, who may be present in the client\'s space, and the contingency plan for technical failure.</p>', 'files' => [], 'due_date' => null]],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Evaluating Teletherapy Outcomes and Limitations',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Teletherapy is not universally appropriate. Clients with significant motor-speech disorders who require hands-on tactile cueing, very young children with limited screen tolerance, or clients with certain sensory or attentional profiles may show better progress with in-person services. A thorough candidacy assessment should weigh the client\'s diagnosis, age, attention span, home support, and technology access before recommending a remote service delivery model, and this decision should be revisited periodically rather than fixed at intake.</p><p>Where teletherapy is appropriate, outcomes research across speech sound disorders, language intervention, and fluency treatment has generally shown comparable effectiveness to in-person service when sessions are properly adapted, though the evidence base is still growing for some populations, such as augmentative and alternative communication users with complex physical needs.</p><p>Clinicians should track functional outcome measures just as rigorously in teletherapy as in person, and should be willing to recommend a hybrid or in-person model if progress stalls or if the remote format is clearly limiting a specific client\'s access to the service.</p>']],
