        'modules' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Foundations of Social Pragmatics',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Pragmatics refers to the social use of language: how a speaker adjusts tone, word choice, and content based on listener, context, and purpose. For autistic individuals, differences in pragmatic skills are a core diagnostic feature, not a secondary consequence of language delay, and can occur even in clients with strong vocabulary and grammar. Key pragmatic skills include initiating and maintaining topics, taking conversational turns, using and interpreting nonliteral language such as idioms or sarcasm, and adjusting communication style for different listeners.</p><p>Joint attention, the shared focus of two people on an object or event along with awareness that attention is shared, is a foundational precursor to later social communication and typically emerges in infancy through gestures like pointing and gaze-following. Differences in joint attention are among the earliest observable markers in autism and are strongly linked to later language and social outcomes, making them an important early intervention target.</p><p>Theory of mind, the capacity to understand that others hold beliefs, intentions, and perspectives different from one\'s own, underlies much of pragmatic competence, including understanding sarcasm, white lies, and why a listener might need more context than the speaker does.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A client has strong vocabulary and sentence structure but struggles to read when a conversation partner is bored or wants to change topics. How would you explain to a parent why this is a pragmatic language difference rather than a behavior problem?']],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Assessing Social Communication',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Formal pragmatic language assessments, such as the CASL-2 pragmatic subtests or the Social Language Development Test, provide standardized scores, but comprehensive assessment should also include structured observation in naturalistic contexts, since many autistic clients can answer questions about social rules correctly while still struggling to apply them in real-time interaction. Clinicians should observe the client across multiple partners and settings, including unstructured peer interaction, since pragmatic performance often varies significantly by context and communication partner.</p><p>Caregiver and teacher interview tools, such as rating scales covering conversational initiation, topic maintenance, and nonverbal communication, add ecological validity that direct testing alone cannot capture. It is also important to assess the client\'s own perspective on their social experiences where possible, since self-report can reveal masking behaviors, social anxiety, or exhaustion that external observation might miss.</p><p>Assessment findings should always be framed in terms of functional impact, identifying which specific skills limit the client\'s participation in valued activities, rather than cataloguing deficits against a neurotypical norm without connecting them to the client\'s own goals.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                        ['question' => 'Why is naturalistic observation important when assessing pragmatic language in autism?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Clients may answer questions about social rules correctly but struggle to apply them in real interaction', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Standardized tests are never valid for this population', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Naturalistic observation replaces the need for caregiver input', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It is faster than formal testing', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Pragmatic competence in real-time, multi-partner interaction often differs from performance on structured test items, so observation reveals functional skill gaps that testing alone can miss.'],
                        ['question' => 'What is joint attention?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Shared focus on an object or event with mutual awareness that attention is shared', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'The ability to sit still during a lesson', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'A formal diagnostic test score', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'The use of eye contact alone', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Joint attention involves both looking at the same thing as a partner and an awareness that the attention is mutually shared, which is more than simple eye contact or shared gaze alone.'],
                        ['question' => 'Why should assessment findings be framed in terms of functional impact?', 'options' => [['text' => 'To connect deficits to the client\'s participation in valued, real-life activities rather than abstract norms', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Because functional framing is required by insurance only', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Because standardized scores are not useful', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Because it avoids the need for goal-setting', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Linking findings to real participation makes goals meaningful to the client and family rather than simply listing deviations from typical development.'],
                    ]]],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Video Modeling and Social Narratives',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Video modeling, in which a client watches a recorded demonstration of a target social behavior before practicing it, is one of the more robustly supported interventions for autistic learners. It leverages visual learning strengths common in autism and allows a skill to be broken into clear, repeatable steps. Video self-modeling, where the client watches themselves performing the target behavior successfully, can be particularly motivating and has shown strong outcomes for skills such as greeting peers or initiating a request.</p><p>Social narratives, often associated with the Social Stories approach, describe a social situation and expected responses in clear, literal, first-person or third-person language. Effective social narratives avoid vague or idiomatic phrasing, focus on describing the situation rather than issuing commands, and are individualized to the client\'s specific context rather than generic templates.</p><p>Both strategies work best as part of a broader intervention plan that includes opportunities for practice with real communication partners and systematic fading of the visual support once the skill generalizes.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'research_paper', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A speech-language pathologist is designing a video-modeling intervention for an 8-year-old autistic client who struggles to join an ongoing peer game at recess. The clinician has access to a tablet, a cooperative peer willing to help film a model video, and the client\'s strong interest in building with blocks. Review what makes video modeling effective (clear steps, appropriate model selection, systematic practice and fading) and outline a plan for this specific scenario, including who should appear in the model video and how you would measure whether the skill generalizes to actual recess.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Peer-Mediated Intervention and Generalization',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Peer-mediated intervention trains typically developing or more socially skilled peers to initiate and sustain interaction with an autistic classmate, rather than relying solely on adult-delivered instruction. This approach capitalizes on the fact that peers are the natural communication partners in the environments where social skills matter most, such as classrooms and playgrounds, and it has shown strong evidence for improving initiations and shared engagement, particularly in inclusive school settings.</p><p>Effective peer-mediated programs train peers explicitly, teaching them specific strategies such as commenting, offering choices, and persisting through a slow or atypical response rather than giving up, and provide adult coaching and feedback during initial sessions before fading support.</p><p>Generalization is the central challenge in social communication intervention: a skill practiced successfully with a clinician in a quiet room does not automatically transfer to a noisy cafeteria with unfamiliar peers. Clinicians should deliberately plan for generalization from the start of intervention by practicing across multiple partners, settings, and materials, and by collaborating with teachers and families to reinforce targets outside of direct sessions.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'graded_quiz', 'content' => ['pass_percent' => 70, 'questions' => [
                        ['question' => 'What is the core idea behind peer-mediated intervention?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Training peers to initiate and sustain interaction with the autistic student in natural settings', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Removing the autistic student from peer settings for individual instruction', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Having an adult shadow the student at all times', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Replacing speech therapy with peer tutoring in academics', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Peers are trained in specific interaction strategies so that social skills are practiced with the natural communication partners present in real environments like classrooms and playgrounds.'],
                        ['question' => 'Why is generalization considered the central challenge in social communication intervention?', 'options' => [['text' => 'A skill learned in one controlled setting does not automatically transfer to different people and environments', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Generalization is not actually important for this population', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Skills generalize automatically once learned with a clinician', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Generalization only matters for articulation goals', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Without deliberate planning across partners, settings, and materials, skills often remain tied to the context in which they were originally taught.'],
                        ['question' => 'What should peer training in a peer-mediated program explicitly include?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Specific strategies like commenting, offering choices, and persisting through atypical responses', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'No training, since peers naturally know how to interact appropriately', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Only general encouragement to be friendly', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Instruction to speak for the autistic student at all times', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Explicit, concrete strategies give peers actionable tools, and adult coaching with feedback ensures those strategies are used effectively before support is faded.'],
