        ],
        'modules' => [
            [
                'title' => 'ASL as a Natural Language',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>American Sign Language (ASL) is a complete, naturally occurring language with its own grammar, syntax, and vocabulary, entirely independent of English. It is not a visual version of spoken English, nor is it universal; British Sign Language and French Sign Language, for example, are distinct languages unrelated to ASL despite their countries sharing a spoken language with English or French respectively. ASL emerged and evolved within Deaf communities, most notably shaped by the founding of the American School for the Deaf in 1817 and the convergence of various home sign systems and French sign language influences brought by Laurent Clerc.</p><p>Linguists such as William Stokoe demonstrated in the 1960s that ASL meets every formal criterion of a human language, including arbitrary symbol-meaning pairings, generative grammar, and the capacity to express abstract ideas, which was a pivotal moment in establishing sign languages as legitimate objects of linguistic study rather than simplified gesture systems.</p><p>ASL syntax often differs structurally from English; for example, topic-comment ordering is common, where the topic of a sentence is signed first and established spatially before comment is added, a structure quite different from typical English subject-verb-object order.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A hearing parent assumes that learning ASL means learning "English with your hands." Based on what you\'ve learned about ASL grammar and history, how would you correct this misconception in a way that is respectful and informative?']],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Fingerspelling and the Manual Alphabet',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Fingerspelling uses a set of 26 distinct handshapes, one for each letter of the English alphabet, to spell out words manually. It is most commonly used for proper nouns such as names and places, for English words that do not have an established ASL sign, for emphasis, and for borrowing technical or specialized vocabulary into a conversation. Fingerspelling is not the primary way ASL conveys everyday meaning; fluent signers rely overwhelmingly on established signs and only fingerspell when necessary.</p><p>Fluent fingerspelling is produced quickly and often blended fluidly between letters, which can make it difficult for beginners to read even once they have memorized the individual handshapes. Reading fingerspelling well is a distinct receptive skill that typically takes substantial practice to develop, separate from expressive production.</p><p>Certain signs have also become "lexicalized" from fingerspelling, meaning a fingerspelled sequence has been absorbed into the language as its own established sign with a fixed, often abbreviated movement pattern, rather than being spelled letter by letter each time.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                        ['question' => 'When is fingerspelling most commonly used in ASL?', 'options' => [['text' => 'For proper nouns, words without an established sign, and borrowed vocabulary', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'As the primary way all everyday conversation is conducted', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Only by beginning learners', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Exclusively for numbers', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Fluent ASL relies mainly on established signs; fingerspelling fills specific gaps like names, places, and words lacking a conventional sign.'],
                        ['question' => 'What does it mean for a sign to be "lexicalized" from fingerspelling?', 'options' => [['text' => 'A fingerspelled sequence has become its own established sign with a fixed movement pattern', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'It means the sign is only used by children', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It means the sign has no connection to English letters at all', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It means the sign must always be spelled letter by letter', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Lexicalized fingerspelling becomes integrated into the language as a standalone sign rather than requiring letter-by-letter spelling each time it is used.'],
