            [
                'title' => 'Non-Manual Markers and Grammar',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Non-manual markers are facial expressions, head movements, and body posture shifts that carry essential grammatical information in ASL, not merely emotional color. For example, raised eyebrows typically mark yes/no questions, furrowed eyebrows combined with a head tilt mark wh-questions (who, what, where), and specific mouth morphemes can modify the intensity or manner of a verb, such as indicating something was done carelessly or with great effort.</p><p>Facial expression and eye gaze are also used grammatically to indicate role shifting, where a signer takes on the perspective of a different person in a narrative by shifting body orientation and gaze, functioning similarly to how a spoken narrator might shift voice and tone to represent different characters. This is a core narrative device in ASL storytelling.</p><p>Because non-manual markers are grammatically obligatory rather than optional in many constructions, a sentence signed with correct handshapes but without the required non-manual marker may be ambiguous or ungrammatical, underscoring that fluent ASL communication depends on far more than hand movement alone.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'research_activity', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'Find a short video clip of a fluent ASL signer (from a reputable Deaf-led educational source) telling a story or asking a question. Without relying on captions, identify at least two moments where facial expression, eyebrow position, or head movement appears to be doing grammatical work rather than just conveying emotion, and describe what each one seems to indicate.', 'requires_submission' => true]],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Deaf Culture and Identity',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Within the Deaf community, a distinction is commonly drawn between "deaf" (lowercase), referring to the audiological condition of hearing loss, and "Deaf" (capital D), referring to cultural identity and membership in a community that shares ASL, values, traditions, and a shared history, often transmitted through Deaf schools and community institutions regardless of degree of hearing loss. Many Deaf individuals view deafness primarily through a cultural and linguistic lens rather than a medical deficit lens, a perspective often called the cultural model of deafness, in contrast to the medical model that frames deafness primarily as something to be treated or cured.</p><p>Deaf culture includes distinct norms around communication, such as direct eye contact, more overt ways of getting someone\'s attention (such as a light tap or waving in the visual field), and value placed on visual storytelling traditions like ASL literature and performance art (such as ASL poetry).</p><p>For professionals working with Deaf clients or families, understanding this cultural framework matters clinically: recommendations framed purely around "fixing" hearing loss without acknowledging Deaf cultural identity and ASL access can be experienced as dismissive, and collaborative, culturally informed practice generally produces better trust and outcomes.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'case_analysis', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A hearing family of a newly diagnosed deaf infant is deciding on a communication approach and has been referred to both a cochlear implant program and a Deaf mentor program that teaches ASL to hearing parents. The family is unsure whether pursuing ASL alongside spoken language options is necessary or might "hold back" spoken language development. Drawing on what you\'ve learned about ASL as a complete language and the cultural versus medical models of deafness, consider how you would discuss these options with the family in a way that respects both their autonomy and the child\'s long-term language access needs.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
                ],
            ],
