    [
        'title' => 'AI and Technology Tools in Modern Speech Therapy',
        'audience' => 'professionals',
        'level' => 'intermediate',
        'short_desc' => 'Evaluate current AI-driven and digital tools used in speech-language pathology, including their realistic benefits and ethical limits.',
        'outcomes' => [
            'Identify categories of technology tools currently used in speech therapy practice and their evidence base',
            'Evaluate speech-recognition-based practice apps for clinical appropriateness',
            'Apply ethical considerations when using AI-assisted tools and client data logging',
        ],
        'modules' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Categories of Technology in Speech Therapy',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Technology tools used in speech-language pathology practice generally fall into a few broad categories. Teletherapy platforms enable synchronous remote service delivery, as covered in telepractice-focused training. Augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) devices and apps, ranging from simple picture boards to sophisticated eye-gaze or speech-generating devices, support clients who cannot rely on speech as their primary communication mode. Data-logging and progress-tracking software allows clinicians to record trial-by-trial accuracy digitally rather than on paper, generating graphs and summaries that support clinical decision-making and documentation for payers.</p><p>A newer category includes speech-recognition-based practice apps, which use automatic speech recognition (ASR) to give clients feedback on their productions during independent practice between sessions. These tools can increase practice volume, which is strongly linked to treatment intensity and outcomes in speech sound disorders, but they are a supplement to, not a replacement for, clinician-directed therapy.</p><p>Clinicians should evaluate any new tool against the same standards used for any clinical material: does it target the client\'s actual goals, is there evidence or at least sound clinical rationale supporting it, and does it fit the client\'s capabilities and context.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => 'A clinic is considering purchasing a subscription to a popular AI speech-practice app for all pediatric clients. What questions would you want answered before recommending its use across your caseload, rather than case by case?']],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Evaluating Speech-Recognition Practice Apps',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Automatic speech recognition technology has improved substantially, but it was originally designed and trained primarily on typical adult speech patterns, which means its accuracy can degrade meaningfully for the exact populations speech therapy serves: young children, individuals with dysarthria or significant articulation errors, and speakers of non-mainstream dialects or accents. A speech-recognition app may fail to recognize a correct production because the child\'s voice quality differs from its training data, or conversely may incorrectly mark a disordered production as correct, giving the client false positive feedback that reinforces an error pattern.</p><p>Because of this, clinicians should personally test any ASR-based tool on productions similar to their specific client\'s speech before assigning independent practice, rather than trusting vendor claims about accuracy. Starting with clinician-supervised use, and monitoring the app\'s feedback against the clinician\'s own judgment over several sessions, helps catch systematic errors before the tool is used unsupervised at home.</p><p>These tools are generally best suited as supplementary drill practice for sounds the client has already begun to master in session, rather than for teaching a brand-new motor pattern, since new skill acquisition benefits most from a human clinician\'s nuanced, adaptive feedback and physical cueing.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                        ['question' => 'Why might a speech-recognition app give inaccurate feedback to a young child with a speech sound disorder?', 'options' => [['text' => 'ASR systems are often trained primarily on typical adult speech and may not generalize well to children or disordered speech', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'ASR apps are always 100% accurate regardless of speaker', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Children\'s voices are too loud for ASR to process', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'ASR apps cannot process any speech sounds at all', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Training data composition affects ASR accuracy, and populations underrepresented in training data, including children and disordered speech, often see reduced accuracy.'],
                        ['question' => 'What should a clinician do before assigning an ASR-based practice app for independent home use?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Test the tool on productions similar to the specific client\'s speech and monitor it against clinical judgment first', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Assign it immediately based on vendor marketing claims', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Avoid ever using any technology-based practice tools', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Use the app only after therapy has been fully discontinued', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Vendor accuracy claims may not reflect performance on a specific client\'s speech, so clinician verification catches systematic errors before unsupervised use.'],
                        ['question' => 'For what type of practice are ASR-based apps generally best suited?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Supplementary drill on sounds the client has already begun mastering in session', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Initial teaching of a brand-new motor pattern with no prior clinician instruction', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Replacing all clinician-directed therapy sessions', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Diagnostic assessment of a new client', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'New skill acquisition benefits from a clinician\'s adaptive, nuanced feedback and physical cueing, while drill practice of an emerging skill is well suited to app-based repetition.'],
                    ]]],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Data Logging and Clinical Decision-Making',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>Digital data-logging tools allow clinicians to record trial-by-trial responses on a tablet or app during sessions, automatically generating accuracy percentages, trend graphs, and session summaries that would take considerably longer to compile by hand. This can support more frequent, data-driven decisions about when to advance a goal, change a cueing level, or address a plateau, and it produces clear documentation for progress reports and payer requirements.</p><p>However, digital convenience does not replace clinical judgment about what data to collect in the first place. A clinician who logs raw percentage-correct without also noting cueing level, context, or error patterns may end up with numbers that look precise but miss the clinical nuance needed to adjust treatment appropriately. Over-reliance on an app\'s default metrics, without customizing data collection to the specific goal, is a common pitfall.</p><p>Clinicians should also consider accessibility and workflow: a data-logging tool that is cumbersome to use mid-session can distract from direct engagement with the client, so ease of use during live treatment should weigh heavily in tool selection, not just reporting features.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'assignment', 'content' => ['instructions' => '<p>Select a digital data-logging app marketed for speech therapy (research one, do not assume features) and evaluate it against three criteria: does it allow tracking of cueing level alongside accuracy, how easy is it to use during a live session without breaking client engagement, and what does it do with the data afterward. Write a short evaluation (200-300 words) with a recommendation for or against adopting it for a pediatric caseload.</p>', 'files' => [], 'due_date' => null]],
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Ethics and Realistic Limits of AI in Clinical Practice',
                'blocks' => [
                    ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>AI-driven tools in speech therapy raise genuine ethical considerations that go beyond simple convenience. Client data, including audio and video recordings used by many AI practice tools, may be processed or stored by third-party vendors, so clinicians must verify a tool\'s privacy practices and data-handling agreements before use, particularly for pediatric clients whose caregivers must give informed consent for this data use. Clinicians remain responsible for understanding, at least at a reasonable level, how a given tool processes client data, rather than treating it as an unexaminable black box.</p><p>It is also important to resist both overhype and dismissiveness about AI tools. AI-based tools are not a substitute for a licensed clinician\'s diagnostic reasoning, individualized treatment planning, or therapeutic relationship, and marketing claims suggesting an app can "replace therapy" should be evaluated with appropriate skepticism. At the same time, dismissing all technology as unhelpful ignores real, evidence-supported benefits such as increased home practice volume and more consistent data collection.</p><p>The most defensible clinical stance treats AI and digital tools as adjuncts that extend a clinician\'s reach between sessions and support documentation, always under clinician oversight, with clear communication to families about what the tool does and does not do.</p>']],
                    ['type' => 'case_analysis', 'content' => ['body' => '<p>A private practice is approached by a vendor offering a free trial of an AI tool that analyzes recorded therapy sessions to automatically generate SOAP notes and suggest treatment targets based on the recording. The clinic is tempted by the time savings on documentation. Consider what privacy and consent issues arise from recording and uploading client sessions to a third-party AI system, what questions you would ask the vendor about data storage and retention, and how you would weigh the documentation time savings against the clinical responsibility to verify the AI\'s suggested targets rather than accepting them uncritically.</p>', 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
                ],
            ],
        ],
    ],
